    {
      "calls": [
        {
          "ID": "toolu_014xWqns9hfVsj8T6PYi8f1q",
          "Name": "submit_read",
          "Input": {
            "assessments": [
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S1:F7",
                  "F26",
                  "S2:F6",
                  "S6:F22",
                  "F30",
                  "S6:F21"
                ],
                "ref": "N1",
                "statement": "The UK met President Trump's reciprocal energy ceasefire by adding pressure on Russia and speeding up air defence for Ukraine, which suggests London is hedging against the ceasefire rather than relying on it.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S1:F7",
                  "F26"
                ],
                "ref": "N2",
                "statement": "As at 13 October, two days after it was announced, there was no sign in the Record that the energy ceasefire had taken hold, since Russia was still reported to be bombarding Kyiv and Ukraine's energy infrastructure.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "S8:F31",
                  "F33",
                  "S9:F35",
                  "S11:F37",
                  "S8:F32"
                ],
                "ref": "N3",
                "statement": "The government is gathering the main pieces of its public-railway programme in Derby (the GBR headquarters, the first apprentices and a £1 billion northern fleet), making the city a deliberate rail-industry hub rather than just a head-office address.",
                "supersedes": ""
              },
              {
                "facts": [
                  "F55",
                  "S13:F51",
                  "F57",
                  "S14:F56"
                ],
                "ref": "N4",
                "statement": "Andy Burnham's approach to Scotland carries his regional-mayor style into Downing Street: he is offering Holyrood cooperation through No10 North on specific jointly funded projects instead of arguments over powers, and the matched £10 million for defence colleges is the first visible result.",
                "supersedes": ""
              }
            ],
            "statements": [
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S1:F7",
                  "S2:F6",
                  "N1"
                ],
                "text": "Ukraine story: on 11 October President Trump announced a reciprocal energy ceasefire between Russia and Ukraine. The very next day, London announced sanctions that now cover more than 90% of Russia's oil-producing capacity. That timing points to the UK hedging against the ceasefire rather than relying on it."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "F26",
                  "N2"
                ],
                "text": "The doubt has a basis. On 13 October, two days after the announcement, Russia was still reported to be bombarding Kyiv and Ukraine's energy infrastructure, and the Record has no sign yet that the ceasefire took hold."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S6:F22",
                  "F23",
                  "S7:F29",
                  "F30"
                ],
                "text": "Military help went the same way. The UK is delivering £45 million of surface-to-air missiles, on top of £1.6 billion already committed for more than 5,000 Lightweight Multirole Missiles. When Zelenskyy visited on 14 October, the Prime Minister set out a further missile package, and the two leaders discussed speeding up defensive support before winter."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S6:F21",
                  "N1"
                ],
                "text": "Ministers have been asked to draw up more sanctions, military aid and financial packages for the coming weeks, so expect more of this. Whether they arrive while the ceasefire is still being talked up is the thing to watch."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S8:F31",
                  "F33",
                  "S9:F35",
                  "S11:F37",
                  "N3"
                ],
                "text": "Railways: the daily Briefing covered the Derby announcements one by one, but together they show a plan. The GBR headquarters (on a brownfield site in the city centre), the first starters on the apprenticeship scheme and a £1 billion northern fleet to be built at Alstom's Derby site all land in one city. That makes Derby a deliberate hub for the publicly owned railway."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "L1",
                  "F55",
                  "F57",
                  "N4"
                ],
                "text": "Scotland: Andy Burnham, Prime Minister since July, is governing much as he ran Greater Manchester, where he chose buses built by Alexander Dennis in Falkirk."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S13:F51",
                  "S14:F56",
                  "N4"
                ],
                "text": "He offered Holyrood cooperation through No10 North and the Scotland Office on specific projects, and the Scottish government has already agreed to match the UK's £10 million for two defence technical colleges. That is the first concrete result of the approach."
              },
              {
                "cites": [
                  "S5:F17",
                  "F16"
                ],
                "text": "Coming up: the UK–EU summit in Brussels on 20 November, which the Prime Minister has already discussed with Ursula von der Leyen in what he called a positive meeting."
              }
            ],
            "stories": []
          }
        }
      ],
